00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.180
Chris: Software developers need to use
good coding principles and practices.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.550
Good coding practices are important in programming
and are achieved by using a variety of principles,

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:17.519
which may initially seem tedious and time
consuming but may be advantageous long term.

00:00:18.149 --> 00:00:22.680
Leah: Cluttered code can cause problems
when it is further extended, and when the

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.370
same code needs to be worked on by a wider
‘Developer team’, so this is one of the reasons

00:00:28.389 --> 00:00:30.650
why there are rules for writing good code.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.540
Let’s take a look at some of the
most common principles used in

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.710
programming and software development:

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.540
Chris: The KISS Principle

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.810
The KISS Principle is a term that is widely used
in a variety of different industries so let’s look

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.500
at what it means especially in relation to coding.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:54.390
KISS is an acronym for a design principle
noted by the US Navy in 1960 and it stands

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:59.720
for ‘Keep it Simple and Straightforward’
and covers many different areas of design

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.100
including engineering and programming.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.299
There are other variations of this including ‘keep
it short and simple’ and ‘keep it simple, stupid’.

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:11.520
Whatever way it is used it means the same thing.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.180
Leah: ‘Keep it simple stupid’ or
even ‘keep it short simple’ are

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.180
most commonly used in programming.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:23.679
By keeping the programming simple, service
and maintenance are much easier and there

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:28.990
are no hard and fast rules regarding the
implementation of code, only that it should

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.770
be simple and therefore easily modified.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.550
There are a number of ways to keep
your code simple these could include:

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:43.369
Chris: variables – keep the scope as small as
possible, don’t reuse variables, declare the

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.030
variables you are using and name them properly.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.770
Functions – keep as small as possible and ‘pure’

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:58.910
DRY – write ‘Don’t Repeat Yourself’ code

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:04.289
YAGNI – Use the ‘You Aren’t Gonna Need
It’ principle – in other words write

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:06.480
the least amount of code that you need

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.949
SOLID – use the SOLID principle.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.120
Leah: There are many reasons for
implementing the KISS philosophy.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:20.730
It may prevent you from wasting time and
effort developing complicated protocols

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.840
which take a long time to write.

00:02:23.559 --> 00:02:28.640
However, the primary benefits, are for
those that may use the software afterwards.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.739
This could include both other
programmers and customers who want

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.380
a product which is simple to use.

00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:36.670
SOLID principles

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.580
Chris: In programming S O L I D is a mnemonic
acronym used for the five design principles

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.730
which are intended to make programs more
‘understandable’, ‘flexible’ and ‘maintainable’.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:51.860
Let’s look at what these are.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.100
S - The Single Responsibility Principle

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:02.810
Leah: The Single Responsibility Principle
[SRP] states that every class or method

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.620
is responsible for one single, separate
part of a program’s functionality.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:12.079
In other words each class should
only have one reason to change.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:17.739
Good practice of SRP will prevent
side-effects from future changes and

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.920
make software easier to implement.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.269
Chris: O – Open / Closed Principle

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.850
Software entities should be open for
extension but closed for modification.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.440
In other words you should be able to add new
functionality without changing the existing code.

00:03:35.039 --> 00:03:41.690
So, for example with OCP, if you change one class
there is no need to change all related classes.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.950
Leah: L – Liskov Substitution Principle

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:51.679
Properties of one original class should
be replaceable with objects built

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:56.239
from classes adapted from the original
without breaking the application.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:03.109
For example, if you had a ‘superclass’
with its own ‘subclasses’, with LSP you can

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:08.300
change the objects of the ‘subclass’ without
affecting its associated ‘superclass’.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:12.059
Chris: I – Interface Segregation Principle

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.659
No client should be forced to
depend on methods they do not use.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:21.909
With ISP it is good practice to have
several client specific interfaces rather

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.490
than one large general-purpose interface.

00:04:25.049 --> 00:04:28.790
This reduces the side effects that could
occur from using a larger interface

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:33.330
as the developers would be focused on
their own client specific interface.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.240
Leah: D – Dependency Inversion Principle

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:43.360
Abstraction should be used to decouple
high-level and low-level modules from each other.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:50.680
This way high-level modules which sustain
complex systems, are not affected by low-level

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.220
modules which provide utility features.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:59.500
High and low level modules should depend on
abstractions which do not depend on details.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:04.529
This means that if the details change,
they do not affect the abstraction.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:11.220
Abstraction is one of the key principles of
object-oriented programming where the programmer

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:18.000
hides all but the relevant data about an object
to reduce its complexity and increase efficiency.

